.\"
.\" nfsd-iomode-advisor(8)
.\"
.TH NFSD-IOMODE-ADVISOR 8 "26 October 2026"
.SH NAME
nfsd-iomode-advisor \- recommend NFSD IO modes for an export
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nfsd-iomode-advisor
.RB [ \-i
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-j ]
.I export
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nfsd-iomode-advisor
measures page cache and memory reclaim metrics on an NFS server.
It then recommends values for the
.B read_io=
and
.B write_io=
export options of
.IR export .
See
.BR exports (5).
.PP
Run the command on the NFS server while clients use the export with a
typical workload.
Run it while the export uses buffered IO for READ and WRITE.
With dontcache or direct IO, the page cache metrics are not meaningful,
and the recommendation is "unknown".
.PP
The command collects these metrics at the start and the end of the
interval, unless noted:
.IP \(bu 2
The bytes that NFSD read and wrote for each export, from
.IR /proc/fs/nfsd/export_stats .
.IP \(bu
The bytes that storage supplied for the READs of the export
.RI ( io_read_storage
in
.IR export_stats ,
on a kernel with
.BR CONFIG_TASK_IO_ACCOUNTING ).
Without this value, the command uses the bytes that the block device
of the export read.
The command divides this value by the bytes that NFSD read.
The result is the miss ratio.
.IP \(bu
Page reclaim and refault counters from
.IR /proc/vmstat .
The command compares the reclaimed memory with the data that the export
read from storage and wrote.
Reclaim can go only as fast as storage fills the page cache, so the
command does not compare it with the memory size.
It also shows the part of reclaim that allocating tasks did themselves
(direct reclaim).
This part increases when kswapd cannot reclaim fast enough.
.IP \(bu
Memory pressure from
.IR /proc/pressure/memory .
.IP \(bu
For each cgroup that reaches its
.B memory.high
or
.B memory.max
limit during the interval, its reclaim, refaults and memory stalls.
These are not system memory pressure.
The command does not count them.
Stalls of different cgroups can overlap, so the remaining memory
pressure is a lower limit.
.IP \(bu
The CPU time of the busiest kswapd or kcompactd kernel thread.
Each NUMA node has its own threads.
.IP \(bu
Each second, Dirty and Writeback memory of the backing device of the
export, from
.IR /sys/kernel/debug/bdi/*/stats .
If the command cannot read these statistics, it uses the values for
all devices from
.IR /proc/meminfo .
The command compares the average with the background writeback
threshold of the kernel
.RB ( vm.dirty_background_ratio
or
.BR vm.dirty_background_bytes ).
.IP \(bu
Free memory and the high watermarks of the memory zones, from
.IR /proc/zoneinfo .
If free memory is far above the watermarks, the page cache has not
filled yet, and the command shows that its recommendation can change.
.IP \(bu
The average READ and WRITE size, from
.IR /proc/net/rpc/nfsd .
.IP \(bu
Optionally, the page cache state of files in the export, from
.BR cachestat (2).
.PP
The command also checks if the filesystem of the export supports
dontcache and direct IO.
If it does not, NFSD uses buffered IO for these modes, and the command
does not recommend them.
Over TCP, NFSD can write with direct IO only if the block device of
the export accepts memory that is aligned to 4 bytes.
Device mapper and MD devices, such as LVM volumes, do not.
For these exports, the command does not recommend
.BR write_io=direct .
.PP
The command reads the
.I rotational
and
.I optimal_io_size
attributes of the block device of the export, from
.IR /sys/block/*/queue .
It does not recommend direct IO on rotational storage.
There, the readahead and writeback of the page cache, which make
fewer and larger requests, are worth more than the cost of page
reclaim.
It recommends buffered IO for WRITEs that are smaller than the optimal
IO size, for example the stripe width of a parity RAID device.
Only the page cache collects these WRITEs into full stripes.
On a system with large pages, it also requires WRITEs of at least
three pages for direct IO.
NFSD writes the misaligned start and end of a WRITE up to page
boundaries with buffered IO.
.PP
For each recommendation, the command shows the reasons.
If the data is not sufficient, the recommendation is "unknown".
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-interval " seconds
Set the measurement interval.
The default is 60 seconds.
.TP
.BI "\-s, \-\-sample\-files " N
Use
.BR cachestat (2)
on the
.I N
largest files in the export.
The command examines at most 10000 files, or 20 times
.IR N ,
to find them.
The default is 0, which disables this check.
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-json
Show the metrics and the recommendation as JSON.
.SH NOTES
The recommendations come from heuristic thresholds.
Use them as a starting point.
Change the IO mode of one export at a time, and compare the metrics
before and after the change.
.PP
The miss ratio includes the readahead and the filesystem metadata
that the READs needed.
A miss ratio clearly larger than 1 shows that readahead reads data that
clients do not request.
.PP
.I io_read_storage
counts only the storage reads that NFSD threads submit.
Some filesystems, such as FUSE filesystems and ZFS, read storage from
their own threads.
For these, the command uses block device reads, if available.
If
.I io_read_storage
shows almost no storage reads but the block device read a large
amount, the command shows that the miss ratio can be too low.
.PP
The block device miss ratio is approximate.
Local applications also cause device reads.
If more than one export is on the same block device, this miss ratio
is for all of these exports.
.PP
The kernel adds an export to
.I /proc/fs/nfsd/export_stats
when a client first uses it.
For an export that no client uses yet, the command reads the export
options from
.IR /var/lib/nfs/etab .
.PP
The kernel keeps the counters of an export when mountd refreshes it.
If an export cache entry appears during the interval with counters
that started earlier, or leaves the cache during the interval, its
traffic in the interval is not known, and the command does not include
it.
.PP
The average IO sizes are for all exports.
The command does not check if READs and WRITEs are aligned for direct
IO.
The memory metrics are for the full system, not for each NUMA node.
.PP
On a system that uses cgroup v1 for the memory controller, the command
cannot separate the reclaim of cgroups at their memory limit.
.PP
The command cannot see the page cache of the clients.
If the client page cache supplies most reads, the server page cache
keeps copies of data that the clients already have.
See
.BR mountstats (8)
on the clients.
.PP
The command must run as root to read the global IO modes in debugfs
and to open the files for
.BR cachestat (2).
.SH FILES
.I /proc/fs/nfsd/export_stats
.br
.I /proc/net/rpc/nfsd
.br
.I /proc/net/rpc/nfsd.export/content
.br
.I /var/lib/nfs/etab
.br
.I /sys/fs/cgroup/*/memory.events.local
.br
.I /sys/kernel/debug/bdi/*/stats
.br
.I /sys/block/*/queue/rotational
.br
.I /sys/block/*/queue/optimal_io_size
.br
.I /proc/zoneinfo
.br
.I /sys/kernel/debug/nfsd/io_cache_read
.br
.I /sys/kernel/debug/nfsd/io_cache_write
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR exports (5),
.BR exportfs (8),
.BR nfsiostat (8),
.BR mountstats (8),
.BR cachestat (2)
